GETPEERNAME(2)      UNIX Programmer's Manual      GETPEERNAME(2)



NAME
     getpeername - get name of connected peer

SYNOPSIS
     #include <sys/socket.h>

     int getpeername(s, name, namelen)
     int s;
     struct sockaddr *name;
     int *namelen;

DESCRIPTION
     Getpeername returns the name of the peer connected to socket
     s.  The namelen parameter should be initialized to indicate
     the amount of space pointed to by name.  On return it
     contains the actual size of the name returned (in bytes).
     The name is truncated if the buffer provided is too small;
     nothing past *namelen bytes is written.

     A UNIX-domain peer's name is the path it was bound to, or
     empty if it was not bound.  The side that called connect(2)
     sees the listener's name.

RETURN VALUE
     0 on success.  On failure -1 is returned and errno is set.

ERRORS
     [EBADF]        s is not a valid descriptor.
     [ENOTSOCK]     s is a file, not a socket.
     [ENOTCONN]     The socket is not connected.
     [ENOBUFS]      Insufficient resources were available in the
                    system to perform the operation.
     [EFAULT]       namelen is a null pointer.

SEE ALSO
     accept(2), bind(2), socket(2), getsockname(2), unix(4)

HISTORY
     The connecting side's view of a UNIX-domain listener's name
     is 4.3BSD-Reno's: 2.11BSD's accepted socket carries no name,
     so the connector's getpeername returns an empty one.



